{"parentUuid":"598b4026-1c3e-4e01-a7ba-b17ae7ee3ab4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01JiWPRf2Y1pdQDau75vTMEV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here's the picture, end-to-end. The log file we redact is the **agent transcript JSONL** that the agent writes to disk; we never touch the agent's file in place, we read it, redact in memory, and persist the redacted form to the checkpoint metadata branch.\n\n## The redactor itself — `redact/redact.go`\n\n`redact.String(s)` is the single chokepoint. It runs six layered detectors and unions all the matched ranges, then replaces them with `REDACTED` (or `[REDACTED_<LABEL>]` for tagged PII):\n\n1. **Entropy.** Find runs of `[A-Za-z0-9+_=-]{10,}`, keep any with Shannon entropy > 4.5 (`entropyThreshold`). Skips matches that begin right after a JSON escape (`\\n`, `\\t`, …) so we don't eat the escape letter.\n2. **betterleaks.** Single shared `detect.Detector` (lazy-init via `sync.Once`) running the ~260 vendor regex rules; we map every `f.Secret` occurrence back to its byte range.\n3. **Credentialed URIs.** `REDACTED`\n4. **DB connection strings.** JDBC URLs, `postgres://...?password=...`, libpq keyword DSNs (`REDACTED), and semicolon-delimited connection strings — each with a `hasSecret` predicate so harmless URLs don't trigger.\n5. **Bounded credential keys.** `(DB|MYSQL|REDIS|…)_PASSWORD=…`, with placeholder filtering (`<password>`, `***`, `${VAR}`, `changeme`, …) so docs don't get hit.\n6. **PII** (opt-in via `ConfigurePII`).\n\n`redact.Bytes` is a thin `[]byte` wrapper over `String`. `redact.JSONLContent` / `redact.JSONLBytes` is the \"field-aware\" version we use for transcripts (next section).\n\n## The compile-time gate — `RedactedBytes`\n\n`checkpoint.CommittedContent.Transcript` and `TemporaryContent.Transcript` are typed `redact.RedactedBytes`, not `[]byte`. You can only build one of those by going through `redact.JSONLBytes(rawBytes)` (does the work) or `redact.AlreadyRedacted(b)` (an assertion used by readers of already-persisted data). This is why every checkpoint write site has a `redact.JSONLBytes(...)` call right before it — there's no other way to satisfy the type.\n\n## How a log file actually flows through\n\nThe main path is the Stop / finalize hook, `manual_commit_hooks.go:2699`:\n\n1. `resolveTranscriptPath(state)` → absolute path to the live agent JSONL (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, etc. — each agent owns its own layout).\n2. `os.ReadFile(transcriptPath)` — raw bytes.\n3. `redact.JSONLBytes(fullTranscript)` inside a `redact_transcript` perf span. Internally `JSONLContent`:\n   - First tries to parse the whole file as one JSON value (pretty-printed OpenCode-style exports).\n   - Otherwise splits on `\\n` and `json.Unmarshal`s each line.\n   - For each parsed value, walks recursively. Skips keys ending in `id`/`ids`, plus `signature`, `filepath`, `file_path`, `cwd`, `root`, `directory`, `dir`, `path`, and objects with `type: \"image*\"`/`\"base64\"` — those are structural identifiers, redacting them would corrupt high-entropy IDs and file paths.\n   - For every remaining string value, runs `redact.String`. Additionally, if we're inside a \"credential-shaped\" object (one that has both a `host` and a `user` field), a generic `password`/`pwd` key triggers redaction even if the value's entropy is low.\n   - Collects `(key, original, redacted)` triples and replaces them in the **original JSON text** via `replaceKeyedJSONValue`, which only substitutes in value position after the matching key — preserves byte-for-byte formatting on every unchanged line.\n   - Lines that fail JSON parse fall back to plain `redact.String`.\n4. On any redaction error, the transcript is dropped to empty (`redact.RedactedBytes{}`); attribution, files-touched, prompts still get written. Hooks don't have a retry path, so partial is better than raw-leak.\n5. Prompts (which are plain strings, not JSON) get `redact.String` directly: `prompts[i] = redact.String(p)`.\n6. The `RedactedBytes` value is then handed to `checkpoint.GitStore.WriteCommitted` / `WriteTemporary` (or the v2 store), which writes the JSONL into the tree on `entire/checkpoints/v1`.\n\nSame flow runs from condensation (`manual_commit_condensation.go:379` via the `redactSessionJSONLBytes` indirection — that's there so tests can stub it) and from `compactAndRedactExternalTranscript` for agents that ship their own compactor.\n\n## The other entry points\n\n- **`doctor bundle`** (`doctor_bundle.go:260`): when zipping diagnostic data, `.json`/`.jsonl` entries go through `redact.JSONLContent`; everything else (logs, command output) through `redact.Bytes`.\n- **`entire explain`** / **`entire attach`**: when re-reading historic transcripts back out of the metadata branch, we re-run `redact.JSONLBytes` defensively (cheap if already redacted, protects older data written before a detector was added).\n- **`checkpoint/committed.go` summary fields**: `Intent`, `Outcome`, `Findings[].Finding`, joined prompt content all go through `redact.String` before being committed.\n\nSo: **one redactor, two shapes** (`String` for plain text, `JSONLBytes` for structured logs), and a **typed payload** that the checkpoint store refuses to accept unless it came out of one of them."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":589,"cache_read_input_tokens":87335,"output_tokens":3459,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":589,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3459,"cache_read_input_tokens":87335,"cache_creation_input_tokens":589,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":589},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CavmxCmofa7nHndEd1LGN","type":"assistant","uuid":"01