{"parentUuid":"ff839bbc-08f5-4b87-ac40-54e1b3ae8c74","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01XXS7WZeHBzx4Kmbmibmmrc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, I think you're right and that's the structurally better shape. The current redactor is allowlist-of-what-to-skip (id, path, signature, …) — which is fragile (any new \"structural\" field added by an agent gets scanned and can false-positive on a UUID) and forces the entropy threshold up high enough that real low-entropy tokens slip through. Flipping it to allowlist-of-what-is-content (user message text, assistant text, tool inputs, tool outputs — driven per-agent, since each agent's JSONL shape differs) lets you drop the entropy threshold meaningfully and stop worrying about UUIDs/hashes in wrapper fields.\n\nThe main tradeoff is **unknown-field risk**: today \"scan everything, skip a few\" leaks-safely by default; \"scan only content fields\" leaks if an agent adds a new content-bearing field and we don't update the extractor. I'd mitigate by keeping the betterleaks regex pass running on the *whole* file (it's high-precision, no entropy false positives) and only restricting the entropy + connection-string + credential-key detectors to known content paths — so the worst case for an unknown field is \"we miss a low-entropy secret in a new schema field\", not \"we miss anything betterleaks would have caught\".\n\nWant me to sketch what the per-agent content-extractor interface would look like (probably an extension of the existing `TranscriptCompactor`), or look at one or two agents' JSONL shapes first to see how cleanly \"content fields\" actually carve out?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3572,"cache_read_input_tokens":87924,"output_tokens":2337,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ep